Relevant columns & their potential usage  
• "age": find the maximum (oldest) and minimum (youngest) user ages.  
• "created_at": filter users registered between 1‑Jan‑2019 and 30‑Apr‑2022 (micro‑seconds epoch ≈ 1546300800000000 – 1651363199000000).  
• "id": count how many users belong to each of the target ages (oldest / youngest).  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (simple → complex), each capped with LIMIT 20 rows to sample the data.

```sql
--Description: 1) Peek at users created in the required interval with their ages.
SELECT "id",
       "age",
       "created_at"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 2) Find the oldest age (maximum) among users who registered in the interval.
SELECT MAX("age") AS "max_age"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 3) Find the youngest age (minimum) among users who registered in the interval.
SELECT MIN("age") AS "min_age"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 4) Show every distinct age and how many users of each age registered in the interval.
SELECT "age",
       COUNT(*) AS "num_users"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
GROUP BY "age"
ORDER BY "age" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 5) Count users whose age equals the oldest age (found via a sub‑query).
SELECT COUNT(*) AS "oldest_age_users"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
  AND "age" = (
        SELECT MAX("age")
        FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
        WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
      )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 6) Count users whose age equals the youngest age (found via a sub‑query).
SELECT COUNT(*) AS "youngest_age_users"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
  AND "age" = (
        SELECT MIN("age")
        FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
        WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
      )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 7) Return both counts (oldest & youngest) side‑by‑side using sub‑queries.
SELECT
  ( SELECT COUNT(*)
    FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
    WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
      AND "age" = ( SELECT MAX("age")
                    FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
                    WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
                  )
  ) AS "oldest_age_users",
  ( SELECT COUNT(*)
    FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
    WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
      AND "age" = ( SELECT MIN("age")
                    FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
                    WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
                  )
  ) AS "youngest_age_users"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 8) Compute the difference (#oldest ‑ #youngest) in one step.
SELECT
  ( COUNT_IF("age" = (SELECT MAX("age") FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
                       WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000))
  - COUNT_IF("age" = (SELECT MIN("age") FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
                       WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000))
  ) AS "difference_oldest_vs_youngest"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 9) Same calculation using a CTE for readability.
WITH date_range_users AS (
  SELECT "id", "age"
  FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
  WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
), extremes AS (
  SELECT MAX("age") AS max_age,
         MIN("age") AS min_age
  FROM date_range_users
)
SELECT
  SUM(CASE WHEN u."age" = e.max_age THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS "oldest_age_users",
  SUM(CASE WHEN u."age" = e.min_age THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS "youngest_age_users",
  SUM(CASE WHEN u."age" = e.max_age THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)
  - SUM(CASE WHEN u."age" = e.min_age THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)     AS "difference_oldest_vs_youngest"
FROM date_range_users u
CROSS JOIN extremes e
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 10) Provide a detailed breakdown plus the final difference using conditional aggregation.
SELECT
  MAX("age") AS "oldest_age",
  MIN("age") AS "youngest_age",
  COUNT_IF("age" = MAX("age")) OVER()   AS "oldest_age_users",
  COUNT_IF("age" = MIN("age")) OVER()   AS "youngest_age_users",
  COUNT_IF("age" = MAX("age")) OVER()
  - COUNT_IF("age" = MIN("age")) OVER() AS "difference_oldest_vs_youngest"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.USERS
WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
GROUP BY "age"
ORDER BY "age" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

